oth computational (DFT) and experimental studies:  **DFT-calculated voltage:** A density functional theory study by Chakrabarti and Thakur (J. Electrochem. Soc. **171**, 080508, 2024; DOI: 10.1149/1945-7111/ad69c8) on Li₂NiPO₄F obtained a voltage of **5.33 V**, along with an electronic band gap of 4.0 eV, a capacity of ~287.3 mAh g⁻¹ (for ~2 Li⁺ extraction), and an energy density of 1531.31 Wh kg⁻¹. The authors noted that while these properties are promising, the large electronic band gap would limit commercial application. As a follow-up, substituting P with Nb to form Li₂NiNbO₄F yielded a lower voltage of 5.01 V, a reduced band gap of 3.64 eV, 215.71 mAh g⁻¹ capacity, and 1080.71 Wh kg⁻¹ energy density — making it an alternative to Li₂NiPO₄F.  **Experimental confirmation:** A 2010 paper in the Journal of the Electrochemical Society (**157**, No. 6, A748–A752; DOI: 10.1149/1.3417068) on "High Voltage Performances of Li₂NiPO₄F Cathode with Dinitrile-Based Electrolytes" demonstrated via cyclic voltammetry and charge/discharge testing that Li₂NiPO₄F has a redox potential of **ca. 5.3 V** against a Li anode. This was enabled by a sebaconitrile-based electrolyte (1 M LiBF₄/EC–DMC–sebaconitrile) that is electrochemically stable above 6 V vs Li/Li⁺.  **Note:** The search results do not explicitly describe Li₂NiPO₄F as having a tavorite-type structure, nor do they contain a dedicated atomistic-simulation study of the tavorite phase (one result references a 2013 atomistic simulation of Li₂CoPO₄F and Li₂NiPO₄F in the Journal of Solid State Chemistry, 204, 329–334, but only structural/defect/ion-transport properties are mentioned, without voltage values). The 5.3 V figure is thus best attributed to the dinitrile-electrolyte experimental work (CV/cycling) and the 5.33 V DFT result of Chakrabarti & Thakur.   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
