nmeta:Language%3DEnglish"}]  ## Summary  Based on the provided search results, **5.3 V vs Li/Li⁺ appears to be the highest voltage reported for a cathode material — and no material in these results demonstrates reversible lithium intercalation above 5.3 V.**  **The 5.3 V benchmark — LiCoMnO₄ spinel** - The spinel LiCoMnO₄ has a discharge plateau up to **5.3 V vs Li/Li⁺** with a capacity of ~145 mAh/g, making it a promising high-voltage cathode. Previously reported samples delivered at most 75% of theoretical capacity with stable cycling under 100 cycles, and typically contained Mn³⁺ (creating an unwanted ~4.0 V plateau). - A University of Maryland (Prof. Wang Chunsheng) / Brookhaven team reportedly synthesized Mn³⁺-free LiCoMnO₄ by a two-step method and **achieved reversible cycling of the material's theoretical capacity for the first time**. Using an electrolyte of 1 M LiPF₆ + 0.02 M LiDFOB in FEC/FDEC/HFE with a 0–5.5 V electrochemical stability window, they demonstrated the first cycle-stable **5.3 V lithium metal battery (Li//LiCoMnO₄)** and a **5.2 V lithium-ion battery (graphite//LiCoMnO₄)**. The material showed two discharge plateaus (5.0–5.3 V and 4.7–4.9 V), 152 mAh/g discharge capacity, and 720 Wh/kg energy density; capacity derives from Co³⁺/Co⁴⁺ redox (Mn is redox-inactive). - Also related: graphite/TiO₂ cells using **anion intercalation into graphite** (not Li intercalation) can raise the charging potential to around **5.3 V vs Li/Li⁺**.  **Materials approaching but not exceeding 5 V** - "5 V" cathodes such as LiNi₀.₅Mn₁.₅O₄ and LiCoPO₄ actually discharge at only ~4.7 V and ~4.8 V vs Li⁺/Li, respectively. - Layered oxides (LiCoO₂, LiNiO₂, Li₂MnO₃) can access reversible Li intercalation at high potentials of **4.3–5 V vs Li/Li⁺**, but with increased interfacial reactivity and capacity fade. - Copper-based polyanionics target the Cu³⁺/Cu²⁺ couple at **5.3 V vs Li⁺/Li**: Li₂CuP₂O₇ shows *non-reversible* oxidation at >5 V (phase unstable at high potential), while Li₄Cu₄O₂(SO₄)₄ shows reversible Li insertion at an average of **4.7 V** but very low capacity (15 mAh/g).  **Record context:** The results indicate that current state-of-the-art cathodes operate beyond known electrolyte stability windows, and electrolyte compatibility (not just cathode voltage) has been the key barrier to stable, reversible operation at ~5.3 V. No reversible Li-intercalation cathode above 5.3 V vs Li/Li⁺ is reported in these search results.   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
