 ~4.0 V - Mn⁵⁺/Mn⁴⁺: well above 5.0 V  These are substantially higher than in layered cathodes (e.g., ~3.8 V average for Ni-rich layered oxides). The high voltages are attributed to the site energy of Li⁺: in spinels Li⁺ occupies tetrahedral sites corner-shared with TM–O octahedra, giving longer Li–TM distances and less Coulombic repulsion (more stable Li⁺) than octahedral sites in layered oxides, raising the equilibrium voltage. The O-2p band edge sits at about 5.0 V, so anion redox is typically not observed in LiMn₂O₄ or LiNi₀.₅Mn₁.₅O₄ even up to 5.0 V.  **DFT-calculated voltages for related spinels** First-principles calculations on spinel LiNi₀.₅Mn₁.₅O₄ give a theoretical potential of **4.7 V**, matching experiment; the redox reaction is carried by Ni²⁺ ↔ Ni⁴⁺, with only a small Mn 3d contribution. This suggests other elements could also serve in 5 V-class spinel materials — substituting Mn by Ti, DFT predicts LiNi₀.₅Ti₁.₅O₄ at **4.9 V**.  **Practical voltage ceiling** For the stable spinel Li₁₋ₓ[Ni₀.₅Mn₁.₅]O₄ (~4.7 V), the operating voltage lies ~0.5 V above the HOMO of liquid-carbonate electrolytes, requiring a more stable electrolyte, a Li⁺-permeable SEI, or an intrinsic kinetic barrier. This spinel is considered an upper limit for host-cathode voltage in a Li-ion battery with carbonate electrolyte, with voltage generally limited to **V < 5 V**.  **Caveat:** None of the provided results report a direct DFT-calculated voltage specifically for LiNi₂O₄ itself; the ~4.6–4.8 V Ni redox values are general spinel values, and the 4.7 V DFT result is for LiNi₀.₅Mn₁.₅O₄. One result also lists a citing paper titled "Simulated synthesis and atomic-level structural characterization of LiNi₂O₄," but without voltage data.   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
