DEC/HFE with LiDFOB additive) enabled the 5.3 V Li-metal cell. - Performance: **720 Wh/kg over 1,000 cycles**, >99% Coulombic efficiency; a **5.2 V graphite||LiCoMnO₄ full cell** delivered **480 Wh/kg for 100 cycles**. - Industry state of the art is noted as only ~4.2 V, so 5.3 V is a major increase (claimed ~3× energy density). - Sources: [xcc.com](https://www.xcc.com/news/detail/2060824), [desn.com.cn](https://desn.com.cn/news/show-717988.html)  ### 2. Patent claims of 5.4 V and 5.5 V cathode charging voltages - Patent **CN102124599A** describes cathodes with high charging voltage vs Li/Li⁺ of 3.5–4.5 V; other embodiments permit **3–5.8 V**, preferably **4.5–5.8 V**. - It explicitly lists cathodes with charging voltages of 4.9, 5.0, 5.1, 5.2, 5.3, **5.4, 5.5**, 5.6, 5.7, or 5.8 V — but no specific cathode material is named for these values in the snippet. - Source: [CN102124599A](https://patentimages.storage.googleapis.com/77/90/39/31a63880b5f586/CN102124599A.pdf#7#5)  ### 3. Known high-voltage cathode materials (>4.5 V vs Li/Li⁺) From a review in *Journal of Power Sources* (1999): - Spinel oxides **Li₂MM′₃O₈** and **LiMM′O₄** (M/M′ = Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, V combinations) operate over 4.5 V. - **LiCoMnO₄**: ~95 mAh/g at a plateau centered on **5.0 V**; among the first single-cell systems to operate over 5 V (with Li₂CoMn₃O₈). - **Li₂FeMn₃O₈**: plateau from **5.0 to 4.8 V**. - Others: **4.7 V** LiNiₓMn₂₋ₓO₄; **4.8 V** LiNiVO₄ and LiCrₓMn₂₋ₓO₄; **4.9 V** LiCuₓMn₂₋ₓO₄. - Source: [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0378775398002043#1)  ### 4. Practical voltage windows - Layered oxides reversibly delithiate within **4.2–4.6 V vs Li/Li⁺**; spinels must be charged **above 4.8 V vs Li/Li⁺** — historically a major hurdle due to electrolyte oxidation ([ECS](https://ecs.confex.com/ecs/236/webprogram/Paper126605.html)). - LiMnPO₄ operates at **4.1 V vs Li/Li⁺** (range cited as 4–5 V) ([Knihovny.cz](https://cpk-front.mzk.cz/SummonRecord/FETCH-LOGICAL-c363t-6c7056f368697c3486c6c8e6ce7f02b6ad36a3ff9240799ff1df22ec9a84675f3?sid=11526917)).  **Bottom line:** The highest demonstrated cathode operation in these results is ~5.0–5.3 V (LiCoMnO₄-based cells), enabled by electrolytes stable to 5.5 V. Values of 5.4–5.5 V appear only as electrolyte stability limits or patent-claimed possible cathode charging voltages, not as demonstrated cathode redox plateaus.   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
