thode ... - ACS Publications - PDF Free Download - AVPO4F (A = Li, K): A 4 V Cathode ... - ACS Publications","url":"https://datapdf.com/avpo4f-a-li-k-a-4-v-cathode-acs-publications.html#1"},{"title":"Lithium Batteries: Science And Technology [PDF] [7iqia77lr2k0] - for Li+ motion, and the structural modiﬁcations on redox reactions are minimal with a volume change of only 3.8 % that contribut...","url":"https://vdoc.pub/documents/lithium-batteries-science-and-technology-7iqia77lr2k0#28"}]  Based on the provided search results, here is what is reported regarding LiNiPO₄F-type fluorophosphate cathodes and their voltages:  **Directly reported voltages for Ni-based fluorophosphates:** - A DFT study of **Li₂NiPO₄F** reported an obtained **voltage of 5.33 V** (along with a band gap of 4.0 eV, capacity of ~287.3 mAh g⁻¹, and energy density of 1531.31 Wh kg⁻¹). - Experimentally, **LiNiPO₄F discharge voltage is demonstrated to be close to 5.3 V**. - More generally, fluorophosphates **Li₂MPO₄F (M = Co, Ni)** are described as having **very high redox voltages of ~5 V**, and Li₂CoPO₄F is noted to display redox activity around 5 V vs Li⁺/Li (higher than olivine LiCoPO₄ at 4.8 V).  **Context on the high voltage:** - The combination of fluoride and polyanionic groups induces highly ionic transition-metal–ligand bonds, contributing to higher working potentials. - However, for Ni-based (and Mn/Co-based) materials, the fluoride/polyanion combination leads to **excessively high redox potentials that are pushed beyond the stability window of conventional liquid electrolytes**. Barpanda et al. showed that LiMSO₄F (M = Mn, Ni, Co) sulfates exhibited no redox activity up to 5 V vs Li⁺/Li, and the same stability limitation is implied for Ni fluorophosphates.  **Caution/note:** The results retrieved do not contain a specific experimental voltage report for *tavorite*-structured LiNiPO₄F specifically; the tavorite references in the results primarily concern LiVPO₄F (noted as a ~4 V cathode wit