ining phases that already contain lithium, starting from lithium deinsertion (oxidation).  **Li₂CuP₂O₇ (copper pyrophosphate)** - Presents a **non-reversible oxidation at high potential (>5 V)**. - Low-temperature synthesis intensifies the reaction (smaller particle size and a conductive carbon coating), **but the phase is unstable at high potential**. - Prior literature (Patoux) studied Li₂CuP₂O₇ in deinsertion involving the Cu²⁺/Cu⁰ couple, not Cu³⁺/Cu²⁺. - The Jahn–Teller distortion of Cu²⁺ deforms the lattice and makes lithium insertion more difficult, likely explaining the scarcity of copper-based battery electrode phases.  **Li₄Cu₄O₂(SO₄)₄ (copper sulfate-type phase)** - A **new phase was isolated**, showing **reversible lithium insertion at an average of 4.7 V**. - However, its **capacity is very low (~15 mAh·g⁻¹)**. - Chemical substitutions with **fluorine, magnesium, or sodium** were attempted to increase lithium mobility.  **Additional context** The simpler oxide LiₓCuO₂ had already proven unstable and cannot reach potentials above 3 V, even over a restricted cycling range.  **Note:** All search results are limited to this single thesis (2016), so the summary reflects only that source; independent corroboration is not available in the provided results.   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
