 is directly reported**. The closest relevant data come from a 2020 DFT study (Alfaruqi et al., *ACS Appl. Mater. Interfaces* **12**(14), 16376–16386; DOI: 10.1021/acsami.9b23367) on mixed transition-metal tavorite/olivine cathodes:  - **Ni-substituted tavorite LiMnPO₄F (LiMn₀.₅Ni₀.₅PO₄F, "LMNPF")**: the calculated Li intercalation voltage **increased to 5.23 V** after Ni substitution (compared to pristine LiMnPO₄F, though the pristine value itself is not given in the snippets). - For comparison, the olivine analogue **LiMn₀.₅Ni₀.₅PO₄ (LMNP)** showed a voltage of **4.27 V**. - The same study reported a lower Li migration barrier of 0.4 eV for LMNPF vs. LMPF, and an ~8% unit-cell volume increase for Mn₀.₅Ni₀.₅PO₄F upon Li insertion (smaller than pristine MnPO₄F).  Separately, a 2011 DFT screening paper (Chem. Mater. 2011, 23, 3854–3862) lists tavorite-structured **LiMn(PO₄)F** among compounds predicted to be directly synthesizable (decomposition energy < 30 meV/atom), but the snippets provided do not include its calculated voltage. (Voltages quoted there for the *fluorosulfate* series (Mn, Co, Ni)(SO₄)F are 4.27, 4.93, and 5.35 V — a related but distinct chemistry.)  **Caveat:** The search results do not contain an explicit experimental or calculated voltage for pristine LiMnPO₄F vs Li/Li⁺; obtaining that value would require the full text of the referenced papers (particularly the tavorite fluorophosphate tables in the Chem. Mater. 2011 study).   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
