[tool_result]Web search results for query: "LiCrxMn2-xO4 spinel Cr3+/Cr4+ redox voltage 4.8 V 5.2 V lithium"  Links: [{"title":"Explanation for the 4.8-V plateau LiCrxMn2-xO4","url":"https://dalspace.library.dal.ca/items/dd6483e6-70a7-47b5-b678-8ea3b9e6e9bb/full"},{"title":"Explanation for the 4.8-V plateau inLiCrxMn2−xO4 | Scilit - Explanation for the 4.8-V plateau inLiCrxMn2−xO4","url":"https://www.scilit.net/publications/18ac5d7870f009657e31fe68ada60de6#1"},{"title":"EXPLANATION FOR THE 4.8-V PLATEAU LICRXMN2-XO4","url":"https://acnpsearch.tweb-dev.unibo.it/singlejournalindex/52485565"},{"title":"Explanation for the 4.8-V plateau in","url":"/hdd/c0710/deepseek/datasets/sci-hub/33800000/libgen.scimag33851000-33851999.zip%20---%2010.1103/PhysRevB.57.5728.pdf#2%231"},{"title":"Transport and electrochemical properties of the LiyCrxMn2−xO4 (0 < x < 0.5) cathode material - Skip to main contentSkip to article","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0378775305000078#1"},{"title":"Transport and electrochemical properties of the LiyCrxMn2−xO4 (0<x<0.5) cathode material","url":"https://www.infona.pl/resource/bwmeta1.element.elsevier-080e0e8c-7839-32eb-b396-7f62eddc4bda"},{"title":"مرکز منطقه ای اطلاع رساني علوم و فناوري - Transport and electrochemical properties of the LiyCrxMn2−xO4 (0 < x","url":"https://search.isc.ac/inventory/10/445467.htm"},{"title":"Revisiting Inverse Spinels: Structure, Electrochemical Properties and Li-ion Diffusion Mechanisms in Li2+xNi2-2xCrxV2O8 (0 ≤ x ≤ 1)","url":"/hf3fs-jd/hdd/deepseek/shared/guankangagi/oatd_pdfs/26/519826.pdf#31#7"},{"title":"Transport and electrochemical properties of the LiyCrxMn2-xO4 (0<x<0.5) cathode material","url":"https://journals.scholarsportal.info/details/03787753/v144i0001/176_taepotlcm.xml&sub=all"},{"title":"Phthalic acid assisted nano-sized spinel LiMn2O4 and LiCrxMn2−xO4 (x = 0.00–0.40) via sol–gel synthesis and its electrochemical behaviour for use in Li-ion-batteries","url":"https://manuscript.isc.ac/Inventory/10/2106418.htm"}]  ## Summary  **Origin of the 4.8 V plateau (Obrovac, Gao & Dahn, *Phys. Rev. B* 57(10):5728–5733, 1998)**  Photoelectron spectroscopy (PES) measurements on LiCrxMn2-xO4 show that the Mn 3d eg-derived d bands lie about 0.5 eV more weakly bound than the Cr 3d t2g-derived bands. In a rigid-band picture, removing lithium first removes electrons from Mn eg (the Mn3+/Mn4+ redox), giving the ~4.1 V plateau. Once those electrons are exhausted, the next available electrons are in Cr t2g, which are more tightly bound by ~0.5 eV — so the cell voltage steps up from 4.1 V to 4.8 V. This 4.8 V plateau corresponds to Cr3+/Cr4+ oxidation.  - The oxidation states are stated as Li+1Crx+3Mn2-x+3Mn+4O4-2, so the voltage step is expected when all (1-x) Mn3+ atoms are oxidized to Mn4+ — i.e., when (1-x) Li has been removed. The authors verified this for single-phase samples that do not exhibit cation mixing. - Structural notes: the lattice constant decreases with increasing Cr content, attributed to the slightly smaller ionic radius of Cr3+ (0.63 Å) vs. Mn3+ (0.66 Å). The extent of cation mixing depends more strongly on preparation temperature than on Cr content. - Electrochemical testing: cells cycled at 30 °C between 2 V and 5.3 V (C/10 charge, C/20 discharge).  **Corroborating electrochemical study (Molenda et al., *J. Power Sources* 144(1):176–182, 2005)**  For LiyCrxMn2−xO4 (0 < x < 0.5) cathodes, charge curves show two visible plateaus separated by a distinct potential jump of ~0.5 V, whose position on the Li-content axis matches the Mn3+ content of the doped material. The lower plateau is assigned to Mn3+ → Mn4+ oxidation and the higher-voltage plateau to dopant Cr3+ → Cr4+ oxidation. The paper proposes schematic diagrams of relative Mn–Cr electronic level alignment. Cr ions were found not to participate in charge transport at low temperatures.  **Additional supporting evidence**  - Sol–gel synthesized LiMn2O4 and LiCrxMn2−xO4 (x = 0.00–0.40) cyclic voltammograms exhibit oxidation/reduction peaks corresponding to both Mn3+/Mn4+ and Cr3+/Cr4+ couples (Thirunakaran et al.).  **Note on the "5.2 V" part of the query:** The provided search results describe the Cr3+/Cr4+ plateau at **4.8 V** (with a step up from ~4.1 V, and cells charged to a 5.3 V cutoff). No distinct 5.2 V plateau is reported in these results; the high-voltage plateau specifically attributed to Cr3+/Cr4+ redox is the 4.8 V plateau.   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
